 "name": "sort_modified_rows",
 "description": "The updates of a sort whose parent modifies rows: which modified rows the sort reports as modified and which it removes and adds. An input table reports every column as modified, so every modified row is placed again. Java looks for a modified row's place among the previous values of all rows, its own included, and keeps the row only if that place is the row itself and no row placed just before it in the same cycle goes to that place. By step, for `asc`: 1 a key that moves before the old one between the same neighbours (removed and added); 2 one that moves after the old one between the same neighbours (modified); 3 an unchanged key (modified); 4 a row that moves to the end; 5 and 6 two rows that exchange their keys (both removed and added); 7 a key that grows in place while a row is added between its old and new value (removed and added); 8 one row that stays and one that moves; 9 a remove, an add and a modify among equal keys; 10 an unchanged key among equal keys beside a row that leaves them; 11 a key that becomes equal to its successor's (modified in `asc`, moved in `desc`); 12 two unchanged keys; 13 removes and a move.",
